  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through the end of October.\n\nLarge scale ridging persists aloft through the end of the week.\nThis results in dry weather and above normal temperatures \nthrough the end of October. These mild conditions will prevent \nany further frost/freeze threats through the end of the week.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure settles over the Tennessee Valley\ntonight while a weakening front approaches from the north, tied\nto low pressure over the Canadian Maritimes. Without much \nforcing or moisture, no precipitation is expected, only an \nincrease in high clouds and a wind shift to the north and \nnortheast. The front will stall and further weaken over the \narea Tuesday, so there won't be much of a drop in temperatures\ncompared to today. Wednesday may be a few degrees cooler with \nextra cloud cover from a combination of warm advection aloft and\nonshore flow at the surface.\n\nStrong surface high pressure will build southward along the Mid\nAtlantic coast in the wake of the weak front. As winds turn\nsoutherly Thursday and skies clear, temperatures will rise\nnotably above normal. That trend will likely continue Friday \nwhen highs in the mid to upper 70s are possible.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Precipitation chances return in early November.\n\nUpper-level ridging overhead breaks down into zonal flow beginning \nFriday and into Saturday. Conditions likely start to become cloudier \nthroughout the day Saturday as troughing develops over the Ohio \nRiver Valley before heading east Sunday into Monday. This \nlikely brings the first chances for precipitation over the Mid- \nAtlantic in over a week, although overall breadth and total \nrainfall from this system remain uncertain at this time. \n\nTemperatures likely drop back to the low-70s over the weekend \nin the wake of another weak front. With overnight lows \ngenerally remaining in the 40s and 50s, this ranges somewhat \nabove normal for the beginning of November. Looking ahead, model\nguidance hints at a second, potentially more potent upper-level\ntrough approaching the area that could bring additional chances\nfor precipitation sometime next week.",
